.TH JANUS-RUN 1 "2026-10-07" "janus 1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
janus-run \- run an annotated shell script with concurrent, grouped, progress-tracked steps
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B janus-run
[\fB\-\-emit\fR]
.I script
[\fIargs\fR...]
.br
.B #!/usr/bin/env janus-run
.RI "(as a shebang interpreter)"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B janus-run
executes an ordinary shell script that has been annotated with
.B #@
comment directives. The directives define a tree of
.B groups
(branches) and
.B steps
(leaves); janus runs the steps with concurrency, a live progress spinner, grouped
output, and per-step status.
.PP
The run mode is selected by the script's
.BR shebang ,
so the invocation is always the same
.RB ( "./script" ):
.TP
.B #!/usr/bin/env janus-run
The kernel execs
.BR "janus-run /abs/path/script [args...]" .
janus transpiles the directives and runs them with the engine.
.TP
.B #!/usr/bin/env bash
The
.B #@
lines are inert comments; the script runs top\-to\-bottom, sequentially. This is the
debugging / plain\-shell mode. To debug an annotated script, change the one shebang
line to bash.
.PP
The annotated source never contains janus runtime calls; the runtime wiring is added
by the transpiler.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-emit
Print the transpiled script to standard output and exit (do not run). Useful for
debugging the transpilation. Flags are explicit\-invocation only and must NOT appear
in a shebang (\fB/usr/bin/env\fR passes at most one argument).
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Display help and exit.
.SH ANNOTATION GRAMMAR
Directives are shell comments beginning with
.BR #@ .
Every
.B group
and
.B step
is closed by its own
.BR "#@ end" .
Groups nest; steps are leaves and never nest.
.TP
.B #@ group [async[=N]]: <header>
Open a group. Without
.BR async ,
children run sequentially. With
.B async
(optionally capped at
.BR N )
a maximal run of adjacent steps runs concurrently.
.TP
.B #@ step [id=X] [depends=a,b] [success=N] [fail=N] [ok=WORD] [err=WORD]: <header>
Open a step. The lines until the matching
.B #@ end
are the step body.
.TP
.B #@ end
Close the most recent open step or group.
.PP
Header text and step bodies are
.B expanded at registration time
in the lexical context where the directive appears, so
.BR $(...) ,
.BR ${var} ,
and loop variables resolve per iteration.
.SS Step attributes
.TP
.B id=X
Explicit step id.
.TP
.B depends=a,b
Declared dependency on sibling step ids.
.B Parsed but not enforced
in this version.
.TP
.B success=N
Context (trailing output) lines to show on success. Overrides
.BR JANUS_CONTEXT_SUCCESS .
.TP
.B fail=N
Context lines to show on failure. Overrides
.BR JANUS_CONTEXT_FAIL .
.TP
.B ok=WORD
Status word shown on success (single word). Overrides
.BR JANUS_OK_TEXT .
.TP
.B err=WORD
Status word shown on failure (single word). Overrides
.BR JANUS_ERR_TEXT .
.SH EXECUTION SEMANTICS
A group processes its children in registration order. A maximal run of adjacent
steps forms one concurrent batch (bounded by
.BR async=N ;
a sync group runs each step singly). A nested group is a
.BR barrier :
the preceding batch finishes before the group starts, and the group finishes before
the following batch starts. Within a batch, steps are launched concurrently but
reported one at a time in registration order; the spinner animates whichever step is
currently being waited on.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
#!/usr/bin/env janus-run
#@ group async: outer
    #@ step: step 1
    echo "working"; sleep 1
    #@ end
    #@ step: step 2
    make -j4
    #@ end
    #@ group async=10: per package
    for pkg in */; do
        #@ step: ${pkg%/}
        ( cd "${pkg}" && build.sh )
        #@ end
    done
    #@ end
#@ end
.fi
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B JANUS_CONTEXT_SUCCESS
Default trailing output lines shown on step success (default: 0).
.TP
.B JANUS_CONTEXT_FAIL
Default trailing output lines shown on step failure (default: 25).
.TP
.B JANUS_OK_TEXT
Default success status word (default: OK).
.TP
.B JANUS_ERR_TEXT
Default failure status word (default: ERR).
.TP
.B JANUS_INDENT
Spaces of indentation per nesting level (default: 2).
.TP
.B JANUS_SPINNER
Spinner glyph set (default: wave-bars). See the spinner module for names.
.TP
.B JANUS_SPINNER_AREA
Visible width in columns reserved for the spinner (default: 5).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
All steps succeeded.
.TP
.B 1
One or more steps failed, or a transpilation / usage error occurred.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I bin/janus-run
Transpiler and interpreter (development tree; sources the runtime from
.IR include/ ).
.TP
.I include/janus.inc
Runtime: registry and the group/step execution engine.
.TP
.I dist/janus
Standalone single\-file build (produced by
.BR "make build" );
the runtime is inlined, so no
.I include/
directory is required.
.SH NOTES
.IP \(bu 2
The runtime requires Bash 4+ (uses
.BR "wait -n" ,
associative patterns, etc.). The transpiler itself also runs under Bash 3.2.
.IP \(bu 2
Color is provided by
.B cprintf
when available on
.BR PATH ,
otherwise output falls back to plain text with markup stripped.
.IP \(bu 2
Step bodies use unquoted here\-documents, so a literal
.B $
in a body must be escaped as
.BR \e$ .
.SH HISTORY
Named after Janus, the two\-faced Roman god of doorways and transitions. The name
reflects the tool's defining trait: a single annotated script has
.B two faces
selected by its shebang \(em an inert plain\-bash script when run under
.BR bash ,
and a concurrent, grouped run under
.BR janus-run .
Group barriers echo Janus's gates; step state changes echo his transitions.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bash (1),
.BR printf (1),
.BR make (1)
